META-COMPLIANT. Same subject, same frontmatter shape, same three level-2 headings, the same single
numbered clause, the same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within 1.06x the byte size of its
counterpart — nothing about the form separates the pair, so the judgement rests on content.

Substance. It states the parent contract correctly: the grep-recoverable token is the PRIMARY, a
position MAY accompany it as a disposable hint and MUST NOT replace it, an ambiguous token is
NARROWED rather than stripped, and a prose description is explicitly named as not a token — which is
the decorated-bare-line shape stated as the test a reader can apply ("whether the reader can recover
the target from the non-numeric part alone").

Authoring. The modal is MUST / MUST NOT / MAY throughout, matching the `## MUST Rules` heading. The
DO/DO-NOT block shows two real citations of the SAME target that differ in whether the non-numeric
part recovers it, so the arms are separable by applying the rule rather than by reading the labels.
The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech and therefore matchable against a transcript. The
`**Why:**` names the consequence of breach — the pointer goes stale before its first use and then
misdirects — rather than restating the obligation.

TRAPS a judge may mis-score. First, this is the longer and denser pole and it argues for extra
characters in every citation, so a judge holding that concise or effort-saving rules are
better-authored prefers its counterpart. Second, its clause heading carries two obligations under
one number — primary-anchor and paired-hint — which can read as under-split; they are the two halves
of one anchoring contract and the parent rule states them the same way. Third, its `**Why:**` runs
long, which can read as over-explanation; the length is consequence, not restatement.
